<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Forcalquier</h1><p class="st-meta">1054 CE – 1209 CE (approx.) · capital Forcalquier</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A county of upper Provence that broke away from the main comital line and was reunited with Provence in 1209.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The county arose from the division of the inheritance of the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a> in the mid-11th century, when one branch kept rights in the north-east around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisteron">Sisteron</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a> valley. In the 12th century it passed to the counts of Urgell, who ruled it as a separate principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> was a land of hill towns and pasture along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a>, controlling routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-of-forcalquier">William IV</a>, the most prominent ruler, held out against the Catalan counts of Provence and the emperors for decades.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1193 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-of-forcalquier">William IV</a>'s granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garsenda-of-forcalquier">Garsenda</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-count-of-provence">Alfonso II of Provence</a>, and on William's death in 1209 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> was reunited with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">County of Provence</a>. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-provence">Raymond Berengar IV</a> ruled both.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-forcalquier.jpg" alt="County of Forcalquier" width="236" height="202"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Provence_1184.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1054 CE – 1209 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Forcalquier</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William IV of Forcalquier, Garsenda of Forcalquier</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd></dl></aside></div>
